            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 border-b-2 border-teal-200 pb-2 mb-6">2. The CHF Connection: Hemodynamic Failure</h2>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6 max-w-4xl">
                In Congestive Heart Failure (CHF), specifically right-sided or biventricular failure, the heart fails to pump blood forward efficiently. This causes blood to back up into the venous system, drastically raising Central Venous Pressure (CVP). Because the thoracic duct empties into this venous system, high CVP creates a "dam," preventing lymphatic drainage and causing severe fluid overload.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-center">
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <canvas id="chfChart"></canvas>
                </div>
                <div class="space-y-4">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 p-4 rounded-r-lg">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-red-800 mb-1">The Starling Principle Failure</h4>
                        <p class="text-sm text-red-700">In CHF, increased venous pressure means increased capillary hydrostatic pressure. More fluid is pushed into the interstitium (edema). Simultaneously, the lymphatic system—which is supposed to clear this fluid—is blocked at the exit point by that exact same elevated venous pressure.</p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-white p-4 rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-slate-100">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Key Hemodynamic Thresholds</h4>
                        <ul class="text-sm text-slate-600 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
                            <li><strong>Normal CVP:</strong> 2-8 mmHg. Lymph flows freely.</li>
                            <li><strong>Mild CHF (CVP 10-15 mmHg):</strong> Thoracic duct must work harder (pump stronger) to overcome the gradient.</li>
                            <li><strong>Severe CHF (CVP > 15-20 mmHg):</strong> Venous pressure exceeds maximal lymphatic pumping pressure. Flow stagnates or stops completely (lymphostasis).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-teal-50 border border-teal-200 p-4 rounded-lg">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-teal-800 mb-1">Port-a-Cath Implication</h4>
                        <p class="text-sm text-teal-700">A lymphatic port-a-cath in a CHF patient could act as a "relief valve." By aspirating lymph from the duct before it reaches the venous bottleneck, we bypass the failing right heart, pulling fluid directly off the congested interstitium.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

