            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-800 border-b-2 border-teal-200 pb-2 mb-6">3. Rheology: Viscosity & Catheter Dynamics</h2>
            <p class="text-slate-600 mb-6 max-w-4xl">
                Lymph is not water. Furthermore, thoracic duct lymph contains <strong>chyle</strong>—lymph from the intestines rich in digested dietary fats (chylomicrons). This makes thoracic duct fluid significantly more viscous and prone to clotting/fibrin deposition than peripheral lymph, directly impacting catheter patency and flow rates according to Poiseuille's Law.
            </p>

            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
                <div class="space-y-6">
                    <div class="glass-card p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-slate-800 mb-2">Poiseuille's Law Context</h4>
                        <div class="bg-slate-100 p-3 rounded font-mono text-center mb-3">Q = (π · ΔP · r⁴) / (8 · η · L)</div>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
                            <strong>Q</strong> = Flow rate. <strong>r</strong> = Catheter inner radius. <strong>η (eta)</strong> = Viscosity.<br><br>
                            Because radius (r) is raised to the 4th power, a tiny reduction in lumen size (due to fibrin sheath) drastically reduces flow. Because Chyle viscosity (η) is higher than water, the required negative pressure (ΔP) to aspirate it via a port is significantly higher.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                    
                    <div class="bg-yellow-50 border border-yellow-200 p-4 rounded-xl">
                        <h4 class="font-bold text-yellow-800 mb-2">The Chyle Challenge for Ports</h4>
                        <ul class="text-sm text-yellow-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
                            <li><strong>Fat Content:</strong> Chylomicrons can adhere to silicone/polyurethane catheter walls.</li>
                            <li><strong>Fibrinogen:</strong> Lymph contains clotting factors. In stagnant flow (like a closed port), it *will* form fibrin sludges.</li>
                            <li><strong>Milky consistency:</strong> Post-prandial (after eating) lymph is thick and milky. Catheter flushing protocols must account for lipid clearance, not just saline.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="chart-container">
                    <canvas id="viscosityChart"></canvas>
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

