                        <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">Direct needle aspiration of fluid from the abdominal cavity (ascites) or pleural space (pleural effusion) when lymph weeps into these cavities.</p>
                        <strong class="text-sm text-slate-800">Limitations/Why a Port is needed:</strong>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">Highly invasive, requires repeated hospital visits. It treats the *symptom* (pooled fluid) after it has already leaked out of the lymphatic system, rather than treating the fluid directly in the plumbing (the thoracic duct).</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Tab Content 3 -->
                    <div id="tab-embolization" class="tab-content hidden">
                        <h3 class="font-bold text-lg text-slate-800 mb-2">Thoracic Duct Embolization (TDE)</h3>
                        <p class="text-slate-600 mb-4">An interventional radiology procedure where the thoracic duct is intentionally blocked (coiled/glued) to treat severe chylous leaks (chylothorax).</p>
                        <strong class="text-sm text-slate-800">Limitations/Why a Port is needed:</strong>
                        <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 mt-1">This *stops* all central lymph flow. It is a destructive procedure used for trauma or specific leaks. It would be catastrophic for a CHF patient who already has fluid overload. A port offers *drainage*, not destruction.</p>
                    </div>

                    <!-- Tab Content 4 -->
